# Analog Devices ADALM-PLUTO (PlutoSDR), and the rest of ADI's USB equipment.
#
# The Pluto is a libusb/IIO device: no kernel driver claims it, so the ownership of the
# /dev/bus/usb/BBB/DDD node is the entire access-control story. Ubuntu ships rules for RTL-SDR,
# SDRplay and osmosdr, but none for Analog Devices, so out of the box the node belongs to root and
# every tool that touches the radio has to run as root. ogn-rf does not, and should not.
#
# Matching the vendor ID rather than a single product ID is deliberate. The radio was observed as
# 0456:B673 running its normal firmware, but a Pluto in its firmware-update / mass-storage mode can
# enumerate under a different product ID — a rule that named only B673 would leave the device
# inaccessible at exactly the moment access matters most, which is when it needs reflashing.
#
# MODE and GROUP make it usable by anyone in plugdev; TAG+="uaccess" additionally grants the user
# holding the seat, which is what makes it work from a graphical session without a group change.
# Installed by scripts/install-pluto-udev.sh.

SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="0456", MODE="0664", GROUP="plugdev", TAG+="uaccess"
